  <p>One exam is a project. Five in a fortnight is a logistics problem. The students who struggle are usually not the ones who know less, but the ones who let one subject eat all their time and ran out of runway for the rest.</p>

  <h2 id="problem">Why multiple exams are hard</h2>
  <p>With several exams close together, two things fight you: limited time, and forgetting. Finish chemistry today and by the time the exam arrives in two weeks, some of it has faded. The solution is to stop thinking in terms of "done" subjects and start thinking in terms of keeping every subject warm while steadily deepening each one.</p>

  <h2 id="combine">One combined timetable</h2>
  <p>Put every exam date into the <a href="/tools/exam-countdown/">exam countdown</a> and see all the deadlines on one screen. Then give each subject a share of your blocks weighted by how soon and how hard it is. The <a href="/tools/study-plan-generator/">study plan generator</a> handles subjects with different dates in one plan, and the <a href="/tools/timetable-maker/">timetable maker</a> lays it out so clashes are obvious before they happen, not after.</p>

  <h2 id="interleave">Interleave your subjects</h2>
  <p>Rohrer and Taylor's experiments show that mixing problem types, rather than drilling one kind at a time, improves later performance, even though it feels harder and slower while you do it. For multiple exams this is doubly useful: two or three subjects a day keeps each fresh and trains you to switch, which is exactly what sitting different papers in a week demands.</p>

  <h2 id="endgame">The final stretch</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Daily touch.</strong> In the last week, give every upcoming subject at least a short session so none goes cold.</li>
    <li><strong>Front-load the hardest.</strong> Do the demanding thinking earlier in the day.</li>
    <li><strong>Test, do not re-read.</strong> Past papers under time for each subject in turn.</li>
    <li><strong>Protect sleep</strong>, which Cepeda-style spacing relies on to consolidate.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>For the single-exam deep dive, see <a href="/guides/how-to-study-for-finals/">how to study for finals</a> and <a href="/guides/study-plan-for-exams/">how to make a study plan for exams</a>.</p>
</Guide>
